  "articleBody": "The Deir Yassin massacre occurred during intense fighting around Jerusalem in early April 1948 (Operation Nachshon context). The village had previously concluded a local non-aggression understanding with nearby Jewish communities; nonetheless, Irgun and Lehi launched a multi-axis assault at dawn. Haganah/Palmach elements later provided support. Killings of civilians—including women, children and elderly—were documented by Israeli and international witnesses; surviving captives were paraded in Jerusalem and later released. Death estimates vary; most research centers near ≈107, with higher claims (≈200–250) appearing in contemporary accounts. The entire village population (≈710 in 1948) was displaced; Deir Yassin's lands were later absorbed into West Jerusalem neighborhoods. No perpetrators were prosecuted; Irgun commander Menachem Begin later became Prime Minister (1977–1983).",
  "keywords": "Massacre, historical, War Crime (1907 Hague); Crimes Against Humanity (modern)",
  "mentions": [
    {
      "@type": "Organization",
      "name": "Irgun (Etzel)"
    },
    {
      "@type": "Organization",
      "name": "Lehi (Stern Gang)"
    },
    {
      "@type": "Organization",
      "name": "Haganah/Palmach (support)"
    }
  ],
